{"assessment":"The source supports a matched pair of normalized Lyapunov scaling laws over the DNS Reynolds-number range Re in [50,1400]. The DNS exponent alpha=0.59 is explicitly reported with uncertainty, and the Kolmogorov mean-field prediction lambda ~ sqrt(Re) (alpha=0.5) is explicitly presented as a competing law on the same normalized domain. Both branches are encoded as (Re/50)^alpha using anchored parameters. The source does not report an amplitude for either law, so the normalized form is used without an unreported prefactor. The source supports the DNS range and the two exponents, so case_authoring_ready is true.","case_authoring_ready":true,"claims":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":389,"line_start":388,"support":"All Lyapunov exponents follow a universal scaling lambda_DNS = lambda_GOY = lambda ~ Re^alpha, with alpha = 0.59 ± 0.04."},{"line_end":47,"line_start":46,"support":"We obtain a precise value of alpha = 0.59 ± 0.04."}],"applicability":"Applies to DNS of the Navier-Stokes equation on a 2π periodic domain with constant energy injection, for Re in [50,1400].","claim_id":"dns_scaling_alpha_0_59","control_domains":[{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":467,"line_start":467,"support":"Reynolds numbers 50 ≤ Re ≤ 1400."}],"control_name":"Re","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"DNS Reynolds numbers in [50,1400].","maximum":1400.0,"minimum":50.0}],"control_names":["Re"],"depends_on":[],"fixed_condition_ids":["dns_domain_2pi","dns_perturbation","intermittent_fluctuations_included"],"mechanism_ids":["lyapunov_scaling_dns"],"oracle_outputs":{"lambda_ratio_to_Re50":{"args":[{"args":[{"var":"Re"},{"param":"Re_ref_50"}],"op":"divide"},{"param":"alpha_dns"}],"op":"power"}},"quantitative_form":"lambda_ratio_to_Re50(Re) = (Re/50)^0.59","readout_names":["lambda_ratio_to_Re50"],"statement":"The DNS Lyapunov exponent follows lambda_DNS ~ Re^0.59 over the DNS Reynolds-number range, normalized to Re=50.","statistical_basis":"Lyapunov exponents estimated from linear fits in the exponential-growth phase; errorbars from fit errors, smaller than symbol sizes.","uncertainty":"Exponent alpha=0.59±0.04; uncertainty convention not specified (source_unspecified)."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":78,"line_start":78,"support":"Ruelle showed that lambda ∝ sqrt(Re)."},{"line_end":395,"line_start":395,"support":"The departure of the measured Lyapunov exponents from a Kolmogorov-like mean field result lambda ~ <gamma_3> ~ sqrt(Re)."}],"applicability":"Mean-field prediction for the Lyapunov exponent in the same DNS context, compared against the measured DNS scaling. This is a theoretical counterfactual, not an observed alternative DNS regime.","claim_id":"kolmogorov_scaling_alpha_0_5","control_domains":[{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":467,"line_start":467,"support":"Reynolds numbers 50 ≤ Re ≤ 1400."}],"control_name":"Re","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"DNS Reynolds numbers in [50,1400].","maximum":1400.0,"minimum":50.0}],"control_names":["Re"],"depends_on":[],"fixed_condition_ids":["dns_domain_2pi","dns_perturbation","monofractal_kolmogorov_mean_field"],"mechanism_ids":["kolmogorov_mean_field_scaling"],"oracle_outputs":{"lambda_ratio_to_Re50":{"args":[{"args":[{"var":"Re"},{"param":"Re_ref_50_kolmogorov"}],"op":"divide"},{"param":"alpha_kolmogorov"}],"op":"power"}},"quantitative_form":"lambda_ratio_to_Re50(Re) = (Re/50)^0.5","readout_names":["lambda_ratio_to_Re50"],"statement":"The Kolmogorov mean-field prediction gives lambda ~ sqrt(Re), i.e., alpha=0.5, on the same normalized domain. This is a theoretical counterfactual, not an observed DNS regime.","statistical_basis":"Theoretical prediction, no statistical fit.","uncertainty":"None reported."}],"controls":[{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":467,"line_start":467,"support":"Vary the coefficient viscosity 10^-3 ≤ ν ≤ 32×10^-3 to obtain Reynolds numbers 50 ≤ Re ≤ 1400."}],"definition":"Reynolds number of the DNS flow, varied by changing viscosity.","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"Reynolds numbers in the range 50 ≤ Re ≤ 1400, obtained by varying viscosity.","maximum":1400.0,"minimum":50.0,"name":"Re","unit":"dimensionless"}],"fixed_conditions":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":278,"line_start":277,"support":"Solving the incompressible Navier-Stokes equation on a 2π triply-periodic domain by using a pseudospectral method."},{"line_end":467,"line_start":467,"support":"Vary the coefficient viscosity 10^-3 ≤ ν ≤ 32×10^-3 to obtain Reynolds numbers 50 ≤ Re ≤ 1400."}],"condition_id":"dns_domain_2pi","statement":"DNS on a 2π triply-periodic domain with pseudospectral method, constant energy injection, and Reynolds numbers in [50,1400]."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":481,"line_start":480,"support":"We use a Gaussian perturbation δu_0(x) = epsilon_0 exp(-(x-x_0)^2/2 sigma_0^2) x-hat with epsilon_0=10^-5, sigma_0=4 dx, x_0=(π,π,π)."}],"condition_id":"dns_perturbation","statement":"Gaussian perturbation with epsilon_0=10^-5, sigma_0=4 dx, localized at domain center, used to initialize system B."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":48,"line_start":46,"support":"We obtain a precise value of alpha = 0.59 ± 0.04 and show that departure from the Kolmogorov mean field result lambda ∝ sqrt(Re) is a consequence of the intermittent fluctuations in the velocity-gradient tensor."},{"line_end":400,"line_start":393,"support":"The departure of the measured Lyapunov exponents from a Kolmogorov-like mean field result lambda ~ <gamma_3> ~ sqrt(Re), is a clear indication that the deviation must stem from intermittent fluctuations and the dominant scaling in the statistics of gamma_3 is due to gamma_3^std ~ Re^alpha."}],"condition_id":"intermittent_fluctuations_included","statement":"The measured departure from the Kolmogorov mean-field result is attributed to intermittent fluctuations in the velocity-gradient tensor, captured by gamma_3^std ~ Re^0.59."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":84,"line_start":75,"support":"Ruelle showed that lambda ∝ sqrt(Re). This result is a consequence of associating the (largest) Lyapunov exponent with the inverse of the smallest time-scale of the flow tau_eta ∝ 1/sqrt(Re). More generally, assuming a Hölder exponent h characterising the flow, it is easy to show lambda ∝ Re^{(1-h)/(1+h)}; indeed taking the monofractal, Kolmogorov limit of a unique h = 1/3 recovers the Ruelle scaling."}],"condition_id":"monofractal_kolmogorov_mean_field","statement":"The Kolmogorov mean-field prediction assumes a monofractal flow with a unique Hölder exponent h=1/3, leading to lambda ~ sqrt(Re)."}],"mechanisms":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":389,"line_start":388,"support":"All Lyapunov exponents follow a universal scaling lambda_DNS = lambda_GOY = lambda ~ Re^alpha, with alpha = 0.59 ± 0.04."},{"line_end":47,"line_start":46,"support":"We obtain a precise value of alpha = 0.59 ± 0.04."}],"equations":["lambda_DNS ~ Re^alpha, alpha=0.59±0.04"],"mechanism_id":"lyapunov_scaling_dns","parameters":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":389,"line_start":388,"support":"All Lyapunov exponents follow a universal scaling lambda_DNS = lambda_GOY = lambda ~ Re^alpha, with alpha = 0.59 ± 0.04."},{"line_end":47,"line_start":46,"support":"We obtain a precise value of alpha = 0.59 ± 0.04."}],"name":"alpha_dns","role":"scaling exponent for lambda_DNS vs Re","uncertainty":"±0.04 (convention source_unspecified)","unit":"dimensionless","value":0.59},{"anchors":[{"line_end":467,"line_start":467,"support":"Reynolds numbers 50 ≤ Re ≤ 1400, so Re=50 is the lower bound of the DNS range."}],"name":"Re_ref_50","role":"reference Reynolds number for normalization","uncertainty":"None reported","unit":"dimensionless","value":50.0}],"statement":"The largest Lyapunov exponent from DNS follows a power-law scaling with Reynolds number: lambda_DNS ~ Re^alpha, with alpha=0.59±0.04. This is the central empirical law extracted from the exponential-growth phase of the decorrelator.","variable_names":["lambda_DNS","Re","alpha"]},{"anchors":[{"line_end":78,"line_start":78,"support":"Ruelle showed that lambda ∝ sqrt(Re)."},{"line_end":395,"line_start":395,"support":"The departure of the measured Lyapunov exponents from a Kolmogorov-like mean field result lambda ~ <gamma_3> ~ sqrt(Re)."}],"equations":["lambda ~ sqrt(Re) = Re^0.5"],"mechanism_id":"kolmogorov_mean_field_scaling","parameters":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":78,"line_start":78,"support":"Ruelle showed that lambda ∝ sqrt(Re)."},{"line_end":395,"line_start":395,"support":"The departure of the measured Lyapunov exponents from a Kolmogorov-like mean field result lambda ~ <gamma_3> ~ sqrt(Re)."}],"name":"alpha_kolmogorov","role":"scaling exponent for lambda vs Re in the Kolmogorov mean-field prediction","uncertainty":"None reported","unit":"dimensionless","value":0.5},{"anchors":[{"line_end":467,"line_start":467,"support":"Reynolds numbers 50 ≤ Re ≤ 1400, so Re=50 is the lower bound of the DNS range."}],"name":"Re_ref_50_kolmogorov","role":"reference Reynolds number for normalization in the Kolmogorov branch","uncertainty":"None reported","unit":"dimensionless","value":50.0}],"statement":"The Kolmogorov mean-field prediction for the Lyapunov exponent is lambda ~ sqrt(Re), i.e., alpha=0.5. This is presented as the competing law that the measured DNS exponent departs from.","variable_names":["lambda","Re"]}],"paper_scope":"The paper studies the Reynolds-number dependence of the largest Lyapunov exponent in fully developed turbulence using decorrelators, DNS of the Navier-Stokes equation, and the GOY shell model. It reports a universal scaling lambda ~ Re^alpha with alpha=0.59±0.04, departing from the Kolmogorov mean-field prediction lambda ~ sqrt(Re). The selected cluster focuses on the DNS-based normalized Lyapunov scaling over Re in [50,1400].","readouts":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":292,"line_start":289,"support":"These difference fields allow us to construct the spatially averaged decorrelator Phi which we plot... We see a convincing exponential regime from which we can extract the Lyapunov exponent lambda_DNS."},{"line_end":376,"line_start":372,"support":"For measurements from the DNSs... we estimate the Lyapunov exponents from a linear fit in the exponential-growth phase of the decorrelators; the errors on such fits yield the errorbars on lambda_DNS."}],"definition":"Dimensionless ratio of the DNS Lyapunov exponent at Reynolds number Re to its value at the reference Reynolds number 50, implied by the reported scaling law lambda_DNS ~ Re^alpha.","name":"lambda_ratio_to_Re50","resolution":null,"statistic":"Extracted from a linear fit in the exponential-growth phase of the decorrelator; errorbars from fit errors.","uncertainty":"No pointwise uncertainty for the derived ratio is reported; the exponent uncertainty is alpha=0.59±0.04 with convention source_unspecified.","unit":"dimensionless"}],"source":{"arxiv_versioned_id":"2505.09538v1","citation":"Aikya Banerjee, Intermittent Fluctuations Determine the Nature of Chaos in Turbulence, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/1dj2-zw28","journal_doi":"10.1103/1dj2-zw28"},"source_ambiguities":[]}
